.PP
The Java binding is the only one that requires external runtime
dependencies.
This is because Java\(cqs standard library did not include Unix domain
socket support until Java 16 (via \f[CR]UnixDomainSocketAddress\f[R]),
and the binding targets Java 11.
junixsocket provides portable AF_UNIX support back to Java 8.
.SH Package structure
.IP
.EX
org.gnu.bash.client
    BashClient                \-\- Main client class (AutoCloseable)
    BashClientException       \-\- Exception hierarchy
    Transport                 \-\- Transport interface
    UnixSocketTransport       \-\- AF_UNIX via junixsocket
    StdioTransport            \-\- Subprocess stdin/stdout
    FdTransport               \-\- Inherited file descriptor
    NamedPipeTransport        \-\- Windows Named Pipe
    NdjsonProtocol            \-\- NDJSON encode/decode

org.gnu.bash.client.channels
    ControlChannel            \-\- Channel 0
    CommandChannel            \-\- Channel 1
    StateChannel              \-\- Channel 2
    ObserveChannel            \-\- Channel 3
    DebugChannel              \-\- Channel 4
    PtyChannel                \-\- Channel 5

org.gnu.bash.client.types
    EvalResult                \-\- Command result POJO
    VarInfo                   \-\- Variable info POJO
    BreakHitEvent             \-\- Debugger break event
    PtyInfo                   \-\- PTY session info
.EE
.SH Architecture
The central class is \f[CR]BashClient\f[R], which implements
\f[CR]AutoCloseable\f[R].
It owns a \f[CR]Transport\f[R] instance and starts a daemon reader
thread at construction time.
.PP
The reader thread continuously reads NDJSON lines from the transport and
routes them:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Request/response messages\f[R] are placed on per\-channel
\f[CR]LinkedBlockingQueue<ObjectNode>\f[R] instances.
When a channel method sends a request, it calls
\f[CR]queue.poll(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS)\f[R] to block until the response
arrives or a timeout occurs.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Server\-push messages\f[R] (observe events, debug break_hit, PTY
output/exit) are dispatched directly to registered callbacks via the
channel\(cqs \f[CR]dispatch()\f[R] method.
.PP
Each channel is exposed as a public final field:
.IP
.EX
client.control   \f[I]// ControlChannel  (channel 0)\f[R]
client.command   \f[I]// CommandChannel  (channel 1)\f[R]
client.state     \f[I]// StateChannel    (channel 2)\f[R]
client.observe   \f[I]// ObserveChannel  (channel 3)\f[R]
client.debug     \f[I]// DebugChannel    (channel 4)\f[R]
client.pty       \f[I]// PtyChannel      (channel 5)\f[R]
.EE
.PP
The reader thread is marked as a daemon thread, so it does not prevent
JVM shutdown.
.SH Connection
\f[CR]BashClient\f[R] provides static factory methods:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Unix domain socket (default)\f[R]
BashClient client = BashClient.connect(\(dq/tmp/bash\-server\-1000/sock\(dq);

\f[I]// Subprocess (\-\-stdio mode)\f[R]
BashClient client = BashClient.connectStdio(\(dqbash\-server\(dq, \(dq\-\-stdio\(dq);

\f[I]// Windows Named Pipe (Cygwin)\f[R]
BashClient client = BashClient.connectNamedPipe(\(dq\(rs\(rs\(rs\(rs.\(rs\(rspipe\(rs\(rsbash\-server\-myname\(dq);
.EE
.PP
All factory methods throw \f[CR]IOException\f[R] on connection failure.
They instantiate the appropriate \f[CR]Transport\f[R] implementation,
call its \f[CR]connect()\f[R] method, and construct the
\f[CR]BashClient\f[R] (which starts the reader thread).
.PP
A \f[CR]BashClient\f[R] can also be constructed directly from a
\f[CR]Transport\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
Transport t = \f[B]new\f[R] UnixSocketTransport();
t.connect(socketPath);
BashClient client = \f[B]new\f[R] BashClient(t);
.EE
.SH Lifecycle \(en try\-with\-resources
\f[CR]BashClient\f[R] implements \f[CR]AutoCloseable\f[R], making
try\-with\-resources the recommended usage pattern:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]try\f[R] (BashClient client = BashClient.connect(socketPath)) {
    client.auth(token);
    EvalResult result = client.eval(\(dqecho hello\(dq);
    System.out.println(result.stdout);
}
\f[I]// close() called automatically\f[R]
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]close()\f[R] method:
.IP "1." 3
Sets the \f[CR]running\f[R] flag to \f[CR]false\f[R], stopping the
reader loop.
.IP "2." 3
Sends a \f[CR]disconnect\f[R] message (ignoring errors).
.IP "3." 3
Closes the transport.
.IP "4." 3
Interrupts the reader thread.
.SH Authentication
.IP
.EX
client.auth(token);
.EE
.PP
Throws \f[CR]BashClientException.AuthException\f[R] on failure.
Check status:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] (client.isAuthenticated()) {
    \f[I]// safe to use all channels\f[R]
}
.EE
