.SH Error handling
All errors are checked exceptions extending
\f[CR]BashClientException\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
BashClientException
    .channel           \-\- Channel that produced the error (\-1 if N/A)
    AuthException      \-\- Authentication failed
    ProtocolException  \-\- Malformed frame or message
    TimeoutException   \-\- Operation timed out (30s default)
    TransportException \-\- Socket or I/O error
    ServerException    \-\- Server returned error response
        .channel       \-\- Channel that returned the error
.EE
.PP
All exception subclasses are static inner classes of
\f[CR]BashClientException\f[R].
This keeps the exception hierarchy contained within a single file.
.PP
Standard try/catch patterns:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]try\f[R] {
    client.auth(wrongToken);
} \f[B]catch\f[R] (BashClientException.AuthException e) {
    System.err.println(\(dqbad token: \(dq + e.getMessage());
}

\f[B]try\f[R] {
    EvalResult result = client.eval(\(dqsleep 60\(dq);
} \f[B]catch\f[R] (BashClientException.TimeoutException e) {
    System.err.println(\(dqtimed out after 30s\(dq);
}

\f[B]try\f[R] {
    client.state.getVar(\(dqNONEXISTENT\(dq);
} \f[B]catch\f[R] (BashClientException.ServerException e) {
    System.err.println(\(dqserver error on channel \(dq + e.getChannel());
}
.EE
.PP
Note that \f[CR]TransportException\f[R] may wrap an underlying
\f[CR]IOException\f[R] as its cause, accessible via
\f[CR]getCause()\f[R].
.SH CONTROL channel (channel 0)
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Authenticate\f[R]
client.auth(token);

\f[I]// Ping\f[R]
client.ping();

\f[I]// Configure\f[R]
client.control.configure(\(dqobserve_level\(dq, 1);

\f[I]// Disconnect\f[R]
client.control.disconnect();
.EE
.SH COMMAND channel (channel 1)
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Simple eval (convenience on client)\f[R]
EvalResult result = client.eval(\(dqecho hello\(dq);
System.out.println(result.stdout);    \f[I]// \(dqhello\(rsn\(dq\f[R]
System.out.println(result.stderr);    \f[I]// \(dq\(dq\f[R]
System.out.println(result.exitCode);  \f[I]// 0\f[R]

\f[I]// Via channel object\f[R]
EvalResult result2 = client.command.eval(\(dqls \-la\(dq);
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]eval()\f[R] method blocks until all three response messages
(\f[CR]stdout\f[R], \f[CR]stderr\f[R], \f[CR]complete\f[R]) have been
received.
stdout and stderr payloads are automatically base64\-decoded.
.SH STATE channel (channel 2)
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Variables\f[R]
VarInfo info = client.state.getVar(\(dqHOME\(dq);
System.out.println(info.value);        \f[I]// \(dq/home/user\(dq\f[R]
System.out.println(info.attributes);   \f[I]// []\f[R]

client.state.setVar(\(dqMY_VAR\(dq, \(dqhello\(dq, List.of(\(dq\-x\(dq));
client.state.setVar(\(dqSIMPLE\(dq, \(dqvalue\(dq);  \f[I]// no attributes\f[R]
client.state.unsetVar(\(dqMY_VAR\(dq);

\f[I]// Functions\f[R]
String definition = client.state.getFunc(\(dqmy_function\(dq);
System.out.println(definition);

client.state.unsetFunc(\(dqmy_function\(dq);

\f[I]// Aliases\f[R]
String aliasValue = client.state.getAlias(\(dqll\(dq);
client.state.setAlias(\(dqll\(dq, \(dqls \-la \-\-color\(dq);
client.state.unsetAlias(\(dqll\(dq);

\f[I]// Traps\f[R]
client.state.setTrap(\(dqSIGINT\(dq, \(dqecho interrupted\(dq);
client.state.unsetTrap(\(dqSIGINT\(dq);

\f[I]// Inspect\f[R]
List<Map<String, Object>> items = client.state.inspect(\(dqvariables\(dq);
\f[B]for\f[R] (Map<String, Object> item : items) {
    System.out.println(item.get(\(dqname\(dq) + \(dq=\(dq + item.get(\(dqvalue\(dq));
}
.EE
.SH OBSERVE channel (channel 3)
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Subscribe\f[R]
client.observe.subscribe(1);

\f[I]// Register callbacks (Consumer functional interfaces)\f[R]
client.observe.onPreCommand(event \-> {
    System.out.printf(\(dq[%d] running: %s in %s%n\(dq,
        event.seq, event.command, event.cwd);
});

client.observe.onPostCommand(event \-> {
    System.out.printf(\(dq[%d] done: %s exit=%d duration=%dms%n\(dq,
        event.seq, event.command,
        event.exitStatus, event.durationMs);
});

\f[I]// Execute commands \-\- callbacks fire on reader thread\f[R]
client.eval(\(dqecho hello\(dq);
client.eval(\(dqls /nonexistent\(dq);

\f[I]// Unsubscribe\f[R]
client.observe.unsubscribe();
.EE
.PP
Observe callbacks are \f[CR]Consumer<PreCommandEvent>\f[R] and
\f[CR]Consumer<PostCommandEvent>\f[R] functional interfaces.
They are invoked on the reader thread, so keep them fast and
non\-blocking.
If heavy processing is needed, submit work to a separate executor.
.SH DEBUG channel (channel 4)
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Enable\f[R]
client.debug.enable();

\f[I]// Status\f[R]
DebugStatus status = client.debug.status();
System.out.printf(\(dqactive=%b mode=%s%n\(dq, status.active, status.mode);

\f[I]// Add breakpoints\f[R]
int bpId = client.debug.addBreakpoint(\(dqcommand\(dq, \(dqecho*\(dq, \-1, \f[B]null\f[R]);
int bpId2 = client.debug.addBreakpoint(\(dqline\(dq, \f[B]null\f[R], 10, \f[B]null\f[R]);

\f[I]// List breakpoints\f[R]
List<Breakpoint> bps = client.debug.listBreakpoints();
\f[B]for\f[R] (Breakpoint bp : bps) {
    System.out.printf(\(dq#%d %s hits=%d%n\(dq, bp.id, bp.kind, bp.hitCount);
}

\f[I]// Break hit callback\f[R]
client.debug.onBreakHit(event \-> {
    System.out.printf(\(dqbreak at line %d: %s%n\(dq, event.line, event.command);
});

\f[I]// Execution control\f[R]
client.debug.doContinue();
client.debug.step();
client.debug.next();
client.debug.finish();
client.debug.skip();

\f[I]// Inspect AST\f[R]
String ast = client.debug.inspectAst();
System.out.println(ast);

\f[I]// Remove breakpoint\f[R]
client.debug.removeBreakpoint(bpId);

\f[I]// Disable\f[R]
client.debug.disable();
.EE
.PP
Note that \f[CR]doContinue()\f[R] is used instead of
\f[CR]continue()\f[R] because \f[CR]continue\f[R] is a reserved keyword
in Java.
.SH PTY channel (channel 5)
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Spawn\f[R]
PtyInfo info = client.pty.spawn(24, 80, \(dq/bin/bash\(dq, \f[B]false\f[R]);
System.out.printf(\(dqpid=%d %dx%d%n\(dq, info.pid, info.rows, info.cols);

\f[I]// Output callback\f[R]
client.pty.onOutput((data, length) \-> {
    System.out.print(\f[B]new\f[R] String(data, 0, length));
});

client.pty.onExit(exitCode \-> {
    System.out.printf(\(dq%nPTY exited: %d%n\(dq, exitCode);
});

\f[I]// Write input\f[R]
client.pty.writeInput(\(dqls \-la\(rsn\(dq);

\f[I]// Resize\f[R]
client.pty.resize(48, 120);

\f[I]// Signal\f[R]
client.pty.signal(\(dqSIGINT\(dq);

\f[I]// Close\f[R]
client.pty.close();
.EE
.SH Callback model
The Java binding uses \f[CR]Consumer\f[R] and \f[CR]BiConsumer\f[R]
functional interfaces for callbacks:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Observe: Consumer<PreCommandEvent>, Consumer<PostCommandEvent>\f[R]
client.observe.onPreCommand(event \-> { \f[B]...\f[R] });
client.observe.onPostCommand(event \-> { \f[B]...\f[R] });

\f[I]// Debug: Consumer<BreakHitEvent>\f[R]
client.debug.onBreakHit(event \-> { \f[B]...\f[R] });

\f[I]// PTY: BiConsumer<byte[], Integer> for output, Consumer<Integer> for exit\f[R]
client.pty.onOutput((data, length) \-> { \f[B]...\f[R] });
client.pty.onExit(exitCode \-> { \f[B]...\f[R] });
.EE
.PP
Callbacks are invoked on the reader thread.
Only one callback per event type is active at a time.
Setting a new callback replaces the previous one.
Pass \f[CR]null\f[R] to unregister.
.SH Threading model
The \f[CR]BashClient\f[R] uses a single daemon reader thread named
\f[CR]\(dqbashclient\-reader\(dq\f[R].
This thread:
.IP \(bu 2
Reads NDJSON lines from the transport.
.IP \(bu 2
Routes request/response messages to per\-channel
\f[CR]BlockingQueue\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Dispatches server\-push messages to callbacks.
.PP
Channel methods block the calling thread with
\f[CR]queue.poll(30, SECONDS)\f[R].
This means:
.IP \(bu 2
Multiple threads can call different channel methods concurrently (each
waits on its own channel queue).
.IP \(bu 2
Two threads calling the same channel concurrently may receive each
other\(cqs responses \(en avoid this pattern.
.IP \(bu 2
Callbacks run on the reader thread, so blocking in a callback will block
all message processing.
.SH Timeout behavior
All blocking channel operations use a 30\-second timeout by default.
If no response is received within this period,
\f[CR]BashClientException.TimeoutException\f[R] is thrown.
.PP
The timeout is implemented via
\f[CR]BlockingQueue.poll(30, SECONDS)\f[R] in the receive path.
There is currently no per\-call timeout override; the 30\-second default
is hardcoded.
.SH Properties
.IP
.EX
client.isConnected()      \f[I]// true if transport is open\f[R]
client.isAuthenticated()  \f[I]// true if auth() succeeded\f[R]
.EE
.SH Complete example
.IP
.EX
\f[B]import\f[R] \f[B]org\f[R].\f[B]gnu\f[R].\f[B]bash\f[R].\f[B]client\f[R].\f[B]BashClient\f[R];
\f[B]import\f[R] \f[B]org\f[R].\f[B]gnu\f[R].\f[B]bash\f[R].\f[B]client\f[R].\f[B]BashClientException\f[R];
\f[B]import\f[R] \f[B]org\f[R].\f[B]gnu\f[R].\f[B]bash\f[R].\f[B]client\f[R].\f[B]types\f[R].\f[B]EvalResult\f[R];
\f[B]import\f[R] \f[B]org\f[R].\f[B]gnu\f[R].\f[B]bash\f[R].\f[B]client\f[R].\f[B]types\f[R].\f[B]VarInfo\f[R];

\f[B]public\f[R] \f[B]class\f[R] Example {
    \f[B]public\f[R] static void main(String[] args) \f[B]throws\f[R] Exception {
        \f[B]try\f[R] (BashClient client = BashClient.connect(
                \(dq/tmp/bash\-server\-1000/sock\(dq)) {

            \f[I]// Authenticate\f[R]
            client.auth(\(dqabcdef0123456789...\(dq);

            \f[I]// Simple command\f[R]
            EvalResult result = client.eval(\(dqecho hello world\(dq);
            System.out.println(result.stdout);

            \f[I]// Set and read a variable\f[R]
            client.state.setVar(\(dqGREETING\(dq, \(dqhello\(dq);
            VarInfo info = client.state.getVar(\(dqGREETING\(dq);
            System.out.println(info.value);  \f[I]// \(dqhello\(dq\f[R]

            \f[I]// Observe\f[R]
            client.observe.onPostCommand(event \-> {
                System.out.printf(\(dqdone: %s exit=%d%n\(dq,
                    event.command, event.exitStatus);
            });
            client.observe.subscribe(1);
            client.eval(\(dqtrue\(dq);

            \f[I]// Brief pause for push events\f[R]
            Thread.sleep(100);
        }
    }
}
.EE
.SH Comparison with other bindings
The Java binding is most similar to the C binding in its blocking call
style, but differs in several ways:
